window.docContents[220].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--A-global-energy-solution-on-whole-space":{"contents":"On all of space, a globally Lipschitz scalar reaction gives a global energy\nconstruction for arbitrary data. The condition  matters here:\n makes composition preserve  even\nthough the domain has infinite measure.\n\nLet  be globally Lipschitz with .\nFor every dimension and every\n, there is a\ncontinuous forward energy trajectory  attaining both data strongly\nand satisfyingfor every , in  and the  dual respectively.\nIt is unique among forward energy-continuous trajectories satisfying these\nintegrated PDE equations with the same data.On every interval  with , the trajectory has the Bochner\nweak-wave formulation. Take any spatial  test  and time test\n with an everywhere-defined derivative on ,\n, and . Its scalar weak equation isThe gradient pairings use the actual Sobolev weak derivatives. No smallness,\ncompact support, or classical regularity of the data is assumed.\n\nThe coercive form  first constructs the\nlinear wave evolution for  through Lax–Milgram and Hille–Yosida.\nThe Lipschitz source  cancels the added mass. Iterated Picard\nestimates construct its Duhamel trajectory for all forward times.\nDense-generator observations give the integrated equations for arbitrary\nenergy data. Conversely, linear weak uniqueness identifies every energy\nPDE solution with that mild trajectory, so fixed-point uniqueness applies\nto the PDE class itself. Time integration by parts gives the displayed\nscalar identity and its initial velocity term.\n\nThis construction asserts the PDE for nonnegative time. Its extension to\nnegative times serves the integrals. The next construction starts from\nbounded smooth scalar data on the real line and obtains classical regularity\ndirectly.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Nonlinear Wave Equations","header":"7.2.4.2. A global energy solution on whole space","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--A-global-energy-solution-on-whole-space"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities":{"contents":"A Sobolev graph norm stores a function and its weak derivatives, but most PDE\narguments need a different form of control.  They may need a stronger\nintegrability exponent, a continuous representative, coercivity of a gradient\nenergy, or an integrable singular quotient.  Four theorem families perform\nthese conversions:\n\n1. Sobolev estimates spend weak derivatives to gain integrability.2. Morrey estimates spend weak derivatives to gain pointwise regularity.3. Poincaré estimates remove the constant mode by imposing zero boundary data.4. Hardy estimates control approach to a distinguished boundary through a\n   singular weight.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings","header":"4.3.4. Sobolev, Morrey, Poincaré, and Hardy Inequalities","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities"}});